---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import AssetLocationLab from "../../components/AssetLocationLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Asset Location: Which Account Holds What"
  description="Same portfolio, same allocation, same tax rates — but which account holds your bonds versus your stocks changes your after-tax wealth. See why tax-inefficient assets belong in tax-advantaged accounts."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Asset location: which account holds what</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Asset <em>allocation</em> is how much you hold in stocks versus bonds.
      Asset <em>location</em> is which account each one sits in — and it's a free
      tax win most investors never take. Hold the same portfolio, but put the
      tax-hungry asset in your sheltered account, and you keep more of it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <AssetLocationLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The rule, and the twist</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Tax-advantaged space is scarce.</strong> A Roth or IRA shelters
          everything inside it from tax. Because you can't shelter it all, it pays
          to fill that space deliberately rather than holding the same mix in every
          account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Traditional shelter → bonds go in.</strong> Bond interest is
          taxed every year at your full ordinary rate, while a broad stock index is
          naturally tax-efficient (growth deferred until sale, dividends taxed
          lightly). So the classic rule shelters the bonds and leaves the index in
          the taxable account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Roth shelter → stocks often go in.</strong> Here's the twist most
          guides skip: because a Roth grows completely tax-free, its wrapper is
          worth most around the <em>highest-returning</em> asset. Sheltering stocks
          usually shields more growth than avoiding the bond drag saves — the
          opposite call. Flip the toggle and watch which asset wins.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Same allocation either way — you haven't changed how much risk you take or
        what you own, only where it lives, which is why asset location is often
        called a free lunch. Real accounts add wrinkles: municipal bonds, state
        taxes, and wanting the right assets in the right place to spend from. Use
        this to see the logic, not as a filing instruction. Educational only, not
        financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="asset-location" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/asset-location" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
